A||||13||DXR_WILL||WHIPPED BY A ECLIPSE
Z||000000||DXR_WILL||07-02-99||12:23 AM||||ONE NIGHT I WAS DRIVING MY DADS FIERO AND I WAS STOPPED AT A LIGHT WHEN I NOTICED THIS CAR APPROACHING IN MY REARVIEW MIRROR.  IT WAS THIS 1992 MITSUBISHI ECLIPSE TURBO.  IT HAD A BIG WHALE TAIL FOR A WING A HUGE 6" TIP ON THE EXHASUST.  UNDER THE HOOD WAS A TURBONETICS T3/T4 TURBO WITH A GREDDY INTERCOOLER, BLOW OFF VALVE, AEM AJUSTABLE CAM SPROCKETS, MSD WITH COIL, AND MAGNACORE WIRES.  I KNOW THIS BECAUSE THE OWNER GOES TO A LOCAL PERFORMANCE SHOP I GO TO FOR MY CELICA AND FIERO.  ANYWAYS I REV TO SEE IF HE WANTED TO RACE, AND HE GAVE ME THE NODE.  AS THE LIGHT TURNED GREEN I TOOK OFF AND IMMEDIATLY TOOK A 1/2 CAR LENGTH UP TO 30 MPH.  THEN I THE SPIN OF A SERIOUS TURBINE AND THE WHISTLE OF A BLOW OFF VALVE AND IT WAS OVER, HE BLEW ME AWAY.  I GOT DUSTED IN THE FIERO, BUT I PLANE ON COMING BACK WHEN MY DAD THROWS IN THE HIGH PERFORMANCE REBUILT KIT AND A TURBO OR SUPERCHARGER.
Z||000001||batboy||07-02-99||10:05 AM||||Man, you must have one cool father! If my dad had ever found out that I did that kind of stuff to his car when I was young, no more car keys or being able to sit down for a week. Not to mention being grounded until I was 50 years old (haha).<p>Sometimes you just have to know when to NOT race. I remember a long time ago (back in the good ol' days) when I had my 68 Chevelle SS 396 and had just blown away my brother's friend in his 75 Camaro. A guy rumbled up beside me in a gorgeous 68 Vette Stingray and told me that if I thought my car was so fast that maybe I ought to race him. I looked over and told him, "I ain't stupid, I know what that L-88 emblem on your hood means!" He just grinned, waved, and drove off. By the way, a L-88 was basically a special near-racing 427 engine producing about 430 hp that the factory stuck into a few select Corvettes.<p>I rarely ever raced anyone until they tried to pick a race with me first. It was lots of fun for me to deflate their big fat egos. I only got beat five times in that Chevelle, by mostly 69 Z-28's (those damn things were a lot lighter and could wrap some unbelievable rpm with those mechanical lifters). But, I also declined a few races too. Like that one Chevy II with the fiberglass front end, wrinkle wall slicks, a big block motor, and a huge honking blower sticking up out of the hood.<p>Just be careful out there, a friend of mine wrapped a Mach 1 around a telephone pole one night when he was racing. He's lucky to still be alive. I almost went to jail one time for drag racing too, man was that scary.
Z||000002||Motorhead||07-02-99||01:52 PM||||Ditto on the Dad comment batboy! The week after I blew away a buddy's '56 Chevy in my Dads car I won a drivers education award at my high school awards assembly, made the folks real proud.<p>To this day, 27 years later, I've never told Dad about that victory.<p>Dad drives a Lincoln Town Car and a Ford F250 now, but I think I detected a gleam in his eye when he drove my Datsun 280 ZX Turbo last year, he hasn't seen the Fiero yet, but that's going to happen this summer, can't wait!<p>By the way, the car he owned back in '72 that beat the Chevy was a Shelby Mustang.  I think he was going through a mid-life crisis at the time.  <p>Be carefull out there Will, street racing can be risky in more ways than just a ticket from the local authorities!  Why not try getting involved in some organized programs?  Autocross, local dragstrips and rallys are all ways to get that EXCITEMENT legally.<p>Just my 2 cents from an old fart who's "been there and done that".<p>Good Luck!
Z||000003||batboy||07-02-99||03:17 PM||||Those Shelby GT's were awesome cars, even to a Chevy/Pontiac guy such as myself. I never ran up against one of them in my wild days, but if I had, I would have declined to race it. They were real light and super fast. You almost never see those Shelby's anymore, they have gotta be worth a mint nowadays. Hey Motorhead, you must of only been about one or two grades ahead of me (class of 1975 here). Our school parking lot was full of Chevy SS cars, GTOs, 442's, Roadrunners, Barracudas, Mustangs, Z-28 Camaros, etc...
Z||000004||87GT||07-02-99||08:14 PM||||I got a story for you folks.  My friend has a  68 Pontiac Firebird Ram-Air 400.  He just had this car restored from top to bottom.  GORGEOUS CAR!  Anyways, he and a few friends(I didnt do it)decided to go to Tampa to meet up with some rice and do a little bit of racing.  Anyways, the cops knew this was gonna happen.  While, they were racing on streets, a helicopter was over head in the distance and could see them racing.  He called into the TPD and told them where the racing was at.  6 cop cars, and a landed helicopter later, they all had $90 dollar tickets and one dude went to jail for the night!!  Ole' John dont race that '68 in Tampa no more, I can tell you that!  This just happened last week!
Z||000005||Motorhead||07-03-99||04:59 AM||||Class of '72 batboy.<p>If you recall, Hertz rent a car bought some of the Shelby's for rentals, problem was, people would rent one for the weekend and take them to the local strip and race them.  The rental deal only lasted a year or two, and that's where my Dad bought his car, from Hertz.<p>Speaking of high school parking lots, the most awesome car in mine was a 'Cuda 340 Six Pack.  I swear the damn thing would suck you in through the hood scoop if you stood too close!<p>Hey. 86GT.  The first time I ever passed that magical 100 MPH mark was in a '68 Firebird that belonged to a friend of my Dads!  
Z||000006||lowCG||07-03-99||10:00 AM||||Thank god for tire and suspension evolution,huh?<br>Have you driven any of those kind of cars lately on the older-tech tires?  Scary.<p>Know of this guy who now,and forever drives a wheelchair,has hand controls in his Aspen SuperCoupe,went faster than he knew how to once on a country road,and smacked a tree.
Z||000007||Motorhead||07-04-99||06:50 AM||||You got that right!<p>I grew up in the mountains of Virginia, and my '70 Roadrunner went great on those flat roads in the valleys, but take it into the twisty roads in the mountains and you took your life in your hands on every curve!  Got a ticket once for crossing a double yellow line when I came around a curve on one of those mountain roads in an untidy manner and met a state trooper! He didn't appreciate dodging the rear end of my Plymouth as we passed!<p>That's how I got into the old English sports cars back then, had a friend with a Triumph TR6, and he dusted me on one of those mountain roads.<p>I do love the way my Fiero handles, it really is old tech, but hooked up with the right tires, I wouldn't trade it for nothing!
Z||000008||Eric||07-04-99||09:40 AM||||Speaking of tires, LowCG, how would you say your A509s rate?  Would you buy them again?  I think my A509s are really good, but I have not really tried out many tire combos.  I found out, AVS tires in the same size are almost the same price.  I think I may try them out next year.  A lot of the autocross guys use them.  Mostly on Integras with stock 5.5 inch wheels to stay in the same class.
Z||000009||BENGAL4||07-30-99||08:59 PM||||Just reading through some of these comments and you guys brought back some memories. A friend of mine had 2 Shelby Mustangs. One was a 68 GT500 convertible and the other was a 67 GT500 hardtop with a 427 sideoiler in it. He bought the convertible in 1979 for $4,000.00 and when he sold it in 1990 (after a ground up restoration) for $63,000.00. He still has the 67. I remember riding to a softball game in the 68, and we were late. We had to going 130 mph on the interstate, and he looked at me and said "look at the inside of the glove compartment lid". I did and there was a sticker that said,"For sustained speeds of 100 mph or more for over and hour, plases see owners manual for tire pressures". What a car. I know that that is a long gone era, but these imports with the backward hat boys are just pretenders. I wish all of them could go back to our drive-in hang out and watch some of the guys roll their cars off of their trailers and go bust them off. By the way, my car back then was a 70 Mach 1 with a 351 Cleveland, shaker hood, factory sport slats and cragar ss wheels. I would love to have that car now. I ended up selling it because I was tired of working on it all week and tearing it up on the weekend. So now, 20 years later, I am restoring a 86GT. I will never sell this one. I will post a page and let everyone know what I have done and am doing to the Fiero.
Z||000010||BluByYou||08-02-99||08:14 AM||||Ok guys ..... here's an interesting memory.  In high school, I started with a '74 Chevy Nova.  Same story as all of you have told - it went fast in a straight line, but the curves were another story.  Anyway, I wound up with a Datsun 240z.  Had a terrible time getting the thing to run right and traded it for (ready for this?) --- a 1969 Opel GT.  Anyone remember those?  Put a Holley carb on that little 1.9 liter and it was like a Fiero.  The thing held the ground almost as well too.  While reading your posts it occured to me that the '69 GT was my first "sports" car.  Yeah, the Datsun should've been, but it was way to troublesome.  Anyway, think of it:  A Black '69 poor-man's corvette.  It was a good summer before, and all through my senior year!   
Z||000011||DJRice||08-02-99||12:40 PM||||The summer after I graduated high school (7 years ago), one of my friends came across a deal on a 67 Camaro Convertible.  The engine (someone had installed a 305) loved to drink the oil so he decided to throw down and buy a real motor.  We worked for about 2 months on that car after work and on the weekends. We dropped in a balanced 350 with pop up pistons, roller rockers, a Wieand Intake, Holley 750 double pumper, Headers, and some Heads that I dont recall what they were but that car was mean.  He only had a three speed tranny at the time.  We eventually swapped it for a 4 spd muncie.<p>It didnt take long for the car to need a set of tires, I can tell you that.  That car was my first experience with travel well beyond 100mph.  Turned out to be about 130+mph.<p>WOW.<br>  <br>Technology and refinement can go a long way, but as much as a love driving my fiero, there is nothing like the raw muscle feeling of a Hurst Shifter trembling with 350HP strapped to it.
Z||000012||batboy||08-02-99||07:24 PM||||BluByYou, wow! I had a 1969 Opel GT once too back when I was 18 and 19, only mine was red. Got more speeding tickets with that car than all the rest of my cars combined. It was a fun car to drive on a mountain road! I called it my poor man's Vette too! I ran the piss out of that car. What memories.
Z||000013||BluByYou||08-03-99||06:49 AM||||BatBoy - maybe that's why we like our Fieros so much, huh?  You are right - what memories.  Those little Opels were a ton of fun.  They had the looks, too.  Ever see one around anymore?  The only ones I've seen are completely roached.  But what the hey, I still have my pics! : )  (I'm glad to hear that I'm not the only one who's ever heard of them, let alone had one, he he)
